  "Privacy-Constrained Scaling Law Inflection" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that privacy-preserving training introduces saturation points in compute scaling. Encompasses: inflection points, compute-privacy trade-offs, and performance saturation.
  Vulnerability: Future algorithmic breakthroughs or novel hardware architectures could potentially decouple privacy from performance, mitigating or eliminating the predicted saturation points and trade-offs.
★ [saf-beliefs-248] (confidence: 0.73)
  "Feedback-Loop Contamination Destroys the Independence Needed for Post-Deployment Verification" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that AI systems whose outputs shape the data they will later be evaluated against make post-deployment output auditing structurally circular.
Encompasses: predictive policing systems generating arrest data that confirms their own predictions, credit-scoring models that deny applications and thereby prevent denied populations from generating default data, pretrial risk tools whose detention recommendations produce the case outcomes used to validate accuracy.
  Vulnerability: Sophisticated causal inference methods—including instrumental variable designs, randomized controlled audits, and natural experiments exploiting policy discontinuities—can in principle recover uncontaminated ground truth even within feedback-affected domains, meaning the circularity is a tractable estimation problem rather than a structural impossibility that 'destroys' verification capacity.
★ [saf-beliefs-137] (confidence: 0.52)
  "Productivity Metrics Miss the Slow Collapse of Institutional Trust" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that short-term AI-driven productivity gains often mask a deeper, irreversible diminishment of trust and societal value. Encompasses: erosion of public trust, increased regulatory burdens from reputational damage, long-term market acceptance challenges, diminished societal value.
  Vulnerability: The claim overstates the irreversibility of trust erosion, as effective governance, transparent AI development, and demonstrable societal benefits can rebuild trust, and some AI applications may even enhance it by improving accuracy or accessibility.

